.TH TOMLLINT 1 "2026-07-31" "tomllint" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tomllint \- check TOML files for syntax errors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tomllint
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.I toml_file
.RI [ toml_file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tomllint
checks one or more files for TOML syntax errors using Python's built-in TOML parser.
It writes diagnostics to standard error and is silent when every input is valid unless
verbose mode is enabled.
.PP
When the sole
.I toml_file
operand is
.BR \- ,
.B tomllint
reads TOML from standard input and uses
.B <stdin>
as the diagnostic filename.
When supplied alongside other operands,
.B \-
is treated as a literal filename.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v , " " \-\-verbose
Write an informational diagnostic for each input that passes validation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every input contains valid TOML, or help was requested.
.TP
.B 1
At least one input contains invalid TOML or could not be opened.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is invalid, such as when no input is supplied.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
TOML syntax errors use the following form:
.PP
.nf
filename:line:column: error: message
.fi
.PP
Files that cannot be opened use line and column zero:
.PP
.nf
filename:0:0: error: reason
.fi
.PP
With
.BR \-\-verbose ,
successful inputs produce:
.PP
.nf
filename:1:1: info: linted successfully
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
Check one file:
.PP
.nf
tomllint pyproject.toml
.fi
.PP
Check multiple files:
.PP
.nf
tomllint pyproject.toml config.toml
.fi
.PP
Check standard input:
.PP
.nf
tomllint \-
.fi
.PP
Report successful validation:
.PP
.nf
tomllint \-\-verbose pyproject.toml
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR python (1)
